Test rg49: type340 escapes that are characters, backspace/super/subscript, and \0
// Each string is a line; the words are at 100 upward, in order.
//   "A\\B"    A, lower shift, \ (052), upper shift, B
//   "A\"B"    A, " (042), B: the quote is in the upper set, so no shift
//   "a\bb"    lower shift, a, backspace 072, b
//   "a\sb"    lower shift, a, superscript 077, b
//   "a\ub"    lower shift, a, subscript 073, b
//   "A\bB"    the lower-set codes get the automatic lower shift ahead of them
//   "A\0B"    the blob, code 0, is a character and is not dropped
//   "A\00B"   the same with two digits
//   "A\52B"   an octal code is stored as it is, with no automatic shift
//   "\U\bA"   after a forced shift the automatic shifts stay off
//   "\\"      the first character, the string starts in upper
//   a backslash at the end of a line is a continuation
// An unknown escape warns, so it is not here: this source has to assemble
// without a word on stderr, as every source the optimizer's sweeps read must.
// The runner tests it separately, with a source of its own.
100/
    type340 "A\\B"
    type340 "A\"B"
    type340 "a\bb"
    type340 "a\sb"
    type340 "a\ub"
    type340 "A\bB"
    type340 "A\0B"
    type340 "A\00B"
    type340 "A\52B"
    type340 "\U\bA"
    type340 "\\"
    type340 "A\
B"
stop
